#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157002 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7043" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}e^{\\textrm{i}t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot (\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t + \\textrm{i}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t + 2\\textrm{i}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(\\cos t - \\sin t) + \\textrm{i}(\\cos t + \\sin t) \\\\ 2\\cos t + 2\\textrm{i}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ 2\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t - \\sin t \\\\ 2\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t + \\sin t \\\\ 2\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos{t} + \\sin{t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-7044" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 2\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2\\textrm{i}t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t)) + \\textrm{i}(\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t)) \\\\ \\cos (2t) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) - \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) + \\sin (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\cos (2t) - 3\\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t) - 2\\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "53243f33-77a1-59f1-bce9-3f084e087593" :question/math-academy-id "e-7045" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}$.*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 =1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now separate this solution into its real and imaginary parts. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{(1 + \\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}(\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i}(e^{t}\\cos t) - e^{t}\\sin t \\\\ e^{t}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}(e^{t}\\sin t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nf(t) = \\cos{t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "9bd3af1e-e569-5cf1-8c4e-97b9ecc35174" :question/math-academy-id "e-7046" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}$.*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 =3+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\textrm{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, we get an eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda}_1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}}_1\n$$\n\nBut we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = 3 + \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand its associated eigenvector is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{(3 + \\textrm{i})t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}(\\cos t + \\textrm{i}\\sin t) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\textrm{i}(e^{3t}\\cos t) - e^{3t}\\sin t \\\\ e^{3t}\\cos t + \\textrm{i}(e^{3t}\\sin t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + \\textrm{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\\n&= w_{1}(t) + \\textrm{i}w_{2}(t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}w_{1}(t) + c_{2}w_{2}(t) \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{2} \\\\ c_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 1\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} - 3\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t - 3\\cos t \\\\ \\cos t - 3\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields 