edge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a91cd0d8-2cd7-56af-abaa-ed476de7883a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155291} {:answer-field/id #uuid "e24a9c33-94d6-5f76-b1bc-2ceba0b7ee6a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155292}] :question/id #uuid "f2ec92c3-9f21-5a42-ab9d-d5296a6ec794" :question/math-academy-id "q-329578" :question/problem "$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$.\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n$x(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}g(t) \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is $λ_{1} = \\textrm{i}$.*\n\n$f(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$$,\n$g(t) =$\n{{field-2}}"}]}
